After cargo run command...

`Hello, world!` means Cargo successfully compiled and ran `src/main.rs`. It exited immediately because it is currently just a regular Rust program, not a web server.

Next, turn it into an Actix-web backend.

### 1. Add Actix-web

From inside `backend`:

```bash
cargo add actix-web
```

### 2. Replace `src/main.rs`

```rust
use actix_web::{get, App, HttpResponse, HttpServer, Responder};

#[get("/")]
async fn home() -> impl Responder {
    HttpResponse::Ok().body("Rust backend is running!")
}

#[get("/api/health")]
async fn health() -> impl Responder {
    HttpResponse::Ok().json(serde_json::json!({
        "status": "ok"
    }))
}

#[actix_web::main]
async fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    println!("Server running at http://localhost:8080");

    HttpServer::new(|| {
        App::new()
            .service(home)
            .service(health)
    })
    .bind(("127.0.0.1", 8080))?
    .run()
    .await
}
```

### 3. Add Serde JSON

```bash
cargo add serde_json
```

### 4. Start the backend

```bash
cargo run
```

This time the terminal should remain running and show:

```text
Server running at http://localhost:8080
```

Open these in your browser:

```text
http://localhost:8080
```

```text
http://localhost:8080/api/health
```

You should get:

```text
Rust backend is running!
```

and:

```json
{
  "status": "ok"
}
```

Keep that terminal open. Use a second terminal to run the React frontend with:

```bash
cd frontend
npm run dev
```
